(Time is the tap-root of most business uncertainties.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(No one can tell what will happen a year, a month, a day, a minute from) Tj T*
(now--the future may bring floods and wars, pestilence and drouth; or it) Tj T*
(may bring great crops and fair weather, happiness and prosperity.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(As business has become more and more complicated, the time element has) Tj T*
(become larger and larger. The time element as we know it does not exist) Tj T*
(in simple barter--a man weaves a piece of cloth and exchanges it for a) Tj T*
(bushel of corn: time is of no account in the transaction. A small) Tj T*
(jobber located in the same territory as refiners buys a small amount of) Tj T*
(sugar today and distributes it to his trade the next--time is) Tj T*
(negligible. A large jobber, buying perhaps for several branch houses,) Tj T*
(or located at points which necessitate a delay of two or three weeks in) Tj T*
(transit, may find it necessary even on a declining market to purchase a) Tj T*
(considerable amount of sugar, and, as a result, weeks may go by before) Tj T*
(his sugar arrives and is sold--time is vitally important.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Time is an element in costs and prices, because over any extended) Tj T*
(period of time many things may happen to influence costs and prices.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(All business planning must deal with Time.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(To the unenlightened business man, Time is a bugaboo--a gambler whose) Tj T*
(cards are stacked and against whom there is no defense. Such a man) Tj T*
(conducts his business from hand to mouth, in constant fear. He is a) Tj T*
(fatalist, taking his profits and losses as if they were gifts or blows) Tj T*
(of Fortune.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The enlightened man works with Time as an impartial, exacting,) Tj T*
(inevitable power for his own good or ill. He shapes his actions and) Tj T*
(enlists the services of Time to prevent catastrophe on the one hand,) Tj T*
(and to enforce prosperity and happiness on the other. Storms may come,) Tj T*
(but so far as his mind may control it, he is "the master of his fate.") Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Cost and Selling Prices) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(That the element of TIME is important in the jobber's business no one) Tj T*
(will deny. He does not base his selling price on cost, but rather on) Tj T*
(the market price. Regardless of his cost, he must sell to meet) Tj T*
(competition. It is equally obvious that the larger his business, or the) Tj T*
(greater his distance from the source of supplies, the more important) Tj T*
(part TIME plays in both his cost and selling prices.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(All jobbers, large or small, are obliged to assume greater risks \(even) Tj T*
(proportionately\) and exercise greater care, than, for instance,) Tj T*
