(Where Buyers and Sellers of Sugar Meet) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The Sugar Exchange is a market place, where buyers and sellers of sugar) Tj T*
(or their representatives meet to trade.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The Exchange provides a concentration point, where, under any market) Tj T*
(conditions, sugar may be bought or sold _at a price_.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(What that price is, is determined by how much sugar is for sale and how) Tj T*
(many people want it. If the supply is large and buyers are few, the) Tj T*
(price will be low. If sugar is scarce and buyers are numerous, the) Tj T*
(price will be high. Or, to put it in another way, when there are more) Tj T*
(sellers than buyers, the market declines; when more buyers than) Tj T*
(sellers, it advances. If the supply and the number of buyers are) Tj T*
(normally well balanced, the price will be determined largely by the) Tj T*
(cost of production and transportation. If events or circumstances) Tj T*
(operate to increase or curtail either the sugar supply or the number of) Tj T*
(buyers, and such events or circumstances follow one after the other) Tj T*
(alternately, the price will fluctuate.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(These are the results of the operation of well-known economic laws.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In the case of all commodities which cannot be bought or sold at a) Tj T*
(common market place \(or exchange\), price fluctuations are usually wide) Tj T*
(and frequent, because no large group ever has common knowledge of) Tj T*
(supply, demand and other factors that govern prices--purchases and) Tj T*
