() Tj T*
(In considering the illustrative examples in this booklet, it should be) Tj T*
(borne in mind that the measure of protection afforded is relative and) Tj T*
(not absolute. The theory of exchange operations is that the exchange) Tj T*
(market will move relatively the same as the market for the actual) Tj T*
(commodity.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(This cannot be strictly true, although the exchange market must of) Tj T*
(necessity follow very closely the actual market, because all the sugar) Tj T*
(must, in the final analysis, come from the actual market. If thrown out) Tj T*
(of parity with the actual market, the exchange market is bound to come) Tj T*
(back eventually.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In the exchange market anyone can buy and anyone can sell. The market) Tj T*
(is subject to many outside influences, and the fluctuations reflect and) Tj T*
(accentuate the varying shades of market opinions of many individuals.) Tj T*
(But in the market for the actual commodity, the quotations are made by) Tj T*
(comparatively few men, which means that there will be less fluctuation.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Therefore, it is obvious that although the exchange market _should_ be) Tj T*
(on a parity with the actual market, the unequal fluctuations of the two) Tj T*
(markets will be constantly throwing them out of parity or "out of) Tj T*
(line.") Tj T*
() Tj T*
(There are times when the market will be so out of line that the _buying_) Tj T*
(of futures should result profitably. At other times, with conditions) Tj T*
(reversed, _selling_ of futures seems obviously advisable. We do not) Tj T*
(claim that jobbers can protect sugar purchases with absolute and exact) Tj T*
(precision. On the basis of long exchange experience, we _do_ believe,) Tj T*
(however, that by a discreet use of the Exchange, and by using the) Tj T*
(market when quotations are _favorably_ out of line, jobbers can do so) Tj T*
(to their decided advantage.) Tj T*
