(advance orders without pre-determining his sugar cost, his profit is a) Tj T*
(matter of guesswork. He is not going to know the cost of his) Tj T*
(manufactured product until he buys his sugar.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Assume that you have contracted to deliver sugar to a manufacturer or) Tj T*
(to any customer at a definite date and a specified price, without) Tj T*
(buying sugar to cover your requirements. If the price of sugar is) Tj T*
(favorable when you deliver it, you are fortunate and net a profit. But) Tj T*
(sugar may have advanced to a point where you are forced to pay such a) Tj T*
(price that your profit is lower than it should be. In fact there may) Tj T*
(not be any profit at all.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(By conservative, wise use of the Sugar Exchange, most of this risk and) Tj T*
(uncertainty can be eliminated and both you and your customer can go) Tj T*
(ahead with your plans with your prices determined through a known sugar) Tj T*
(cost.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Suppose that in March or April, for example, the market appears strong) Tj T*
(and you find that some of your manufacturing customers are anxious to) Tj T*
(be assured of an adequate supply of sugar at a definite price. In such) Tj T*
(a case, if these advance orders called for a sufficient volume, and) Tj T*
(provided Exchange prices were favorable, you could take care of your) Tj T*
(trade's future requirements at a fixed price, without yourself taking a) Tj T*
(speculative position. We also believe that buyers making these) Tj T*
(arrangements with any of their trade would be justified in requesting) Tj T*
(the same proportionate marginal protection which it is necessary for) Tj T*
(jobbers themselves to give the seller on the Exchange. There will no) Tj T*
(doubt be many occasions when it would be worth while to solicit orders) Tj T*
(on this basis.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(With your own sugar cost fixed by the use of the Exchange, you could) Tj T*
(take proper care of these buyers without worrying about subsequent) Tj T*
(fluctuations of the market, as you would know that your sugar cost) Tj T*
(would be about the price paid for your futures which, let us say, is) Tj T*
(6.00. \(See Chart 4.\)) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The market may advance so that by September, sugar is selling at 8.00.) Tj T*
(\(You are now making deliveries to your trade as contracted\). So you) Tj T*
(sell your futures at 8.00, go into the market and buy actual sugar for) Tj T*
(about the same figure, assuming, of course, that actual sugar has also) Tj T*
(advanced in relative proportion, which is likely. You pay 2.00 more for) Tj T*
(your actual sugar than you had figured but you have profited to the) Tj T*
(extent of 2.00 on the sale of futures. Profit and loss cancel each) Tj T*
(other and you have your sugar at 6.00. In other words, although the) Tj T*
(market is now 8.00 you are delivering 6.00 sugar to your customers,) Tj T*
(with a profit to yourself.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(If the market declines after your original purchase at 6.00 so that in) Tj T*
(September sugar is selling at 4.00, you will sell your futures at 4.00,) Tj T*
(taking a loss of 2.00. But you will buy your actual sugar at about) Tj T*
(4.00, also, which is 2.00 lower than you planned for. This gain of) Tj T*
(2.00, while not to be termed an actual profit, may certainly be) Tj T*
