(When are Refiners' Prices and Exchange Quotations in line?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Since exchange quotations for refined sugar futures are net cash) Tj T*
(ex-exchange-licensed warehouse, Chicago, while refiners' quotations are) Tj T*
(f.o.b. refinery, less 2% for cash, it is obvious that there must be a) Tj T*
(difference between refiners' prices and exchange quotations.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(It is equally obvious that the differential should approximate the) Tj T*
(freight rate between Chicago and the Seaboard, where the refiners are) Tj T*
(located, with allowance also for the cash discount. When the markets) Tj T*
(are in line such is the case. Conversely, when the differential is) Tj T*
(higher or lower, the markets are out of line.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Therefore, in order to tell whether the markets are out of line, or to) Tj T*
(what extent, it is necessary to determine on a differential to) Tj T*
(represent the normal difference between the two markets. There is no) Tj T*
(one figure, however, that will satisfy all conditions at all times, for) Tj T*
(the reason that there are various freight rates between the Seaboard) Tj T*
(and Chicago. It is inaccurate, for instance, to use 63в as the basis) Tj T*
(for the normal differential. The 63в rate is one rate--the all-rail) Tj T*
(freight rate from New York to Chicago.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
